{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/2026-07-22-the-secret-life-of-sauce/audio/main-c3398b6f7c52.mp3","durationSec":292.224,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":8.34,"text":"Some of the world's most important sauces get their depth from fermentation — a slow, natural process in which microbes transform ingredients and create new flavors."},{"start":9.44,"end":14.56,"text":"Fermentation can preserve food, but it can also build tastes that ordinary cooking does not produce."},{"start":14.56,"end":17.56,"text":"Kikkoman soy sauce is a clear example."},{"start":18.54,"end":20.98,"text":"Soybeans and roasted wheat are combined with koji mold."},{"start":21.42,"end":25.18,"text":"The koji supplies enzymes that begin breaking down proteins and starches."},{"start":25.18,"end":28.92,"text":"Salt water is then added to form a mash called moromi."},{"start":31,"end":36.52,"text":"Over several months, microorganisms continue changing this mixture as it ferments and ages."},{"start":37.28,"end":41.96,"text":"The liquid develops a rich brown color, a salty taste, and a complex aroma."},{"start":42.92,"end":47.76,"text":"This patient transformation explains why fermented soy sauce tastes like more than salt."},{"start":47.76,"end":53.24,"text":"Kikkoman describes how fermentation creates the sauce's flavor, aroma, and color together."},{"start":53.62,"end":61.54,"text":"The result includes umami: the deep savory quality that can make plain rice, tofu, or vegetables feel more satisfying."},{"start":63.26,"end":65.48,"text":"Not all sauces are born where we expect them."},{"start":66.2,"end":71.76,"text":"Ketchup is a good example of how a sauce can travel through several cultures and change completely along the way."},{"start":72.42,"end":73.82,"text":"The story begins in Asia."},{"start":74.5,"end":78.26,"text":"Early versions were fermented fish sauces used to add depth to food."},{"start":79.02,"end":84.68,"text":"British sailors encountered related sauces through Chinese and Indonesian traders and carried the idea home."},{"start":85.44,"end":90.46,"text":"British cooks then experimented with ingredients they could find locally, including mushrooms and walnuts."},{"start":91.14,"end":94,"text":"Their ketchups were dark, thin, and strongly savory."},{"start":94.66,"end":98.76,"text":"In the nineteenth century, tomato ketchup grew popular in the United States."},{"start":99.1,"end":106.16,"text":"Producers made it thicker and added vinegar, sugar, and spices, creating the sweet, sour sauce many people recognize today."},{"start":106.88,"end":113.12,"text":"This version later influenced tomato farming, factory processing, and even debates about food safety."},{"start":113.86,"end":116.62,"text":"Ketchup's journey shows that a sauce is not fixed."},{"start":117.24,"end":120.06,"text":"It adapts to local ingredients, tastes, and needs."},{"start":121.46,"end":127.4,"text":"The familiar red bottle is therefore not the beginning of the story; it is one stop on a much longer route."},{"start":128.76,"end":134.98,"text":"Japan has a remarkable history of adopting foreign food ideas and reshaping them until they feel familiar."},{"start":134.98,"end":138.64,"text":"Curry sauce and mayonnaise show two different paths."},{"start":139.52,"end":152.12,"text":"One theory recorded by Japan's Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries links Japanese curry rice to a curry-flavored stew used in the British Royal Navy and later the Japanese Navy during the Meiji era."},{"start":152.56,"end":156.9,"text":"In Japan, the thick sauce was served over rice with meat and vegetables."},{"start":157.94,"end":164.82,"text":"Retired sailors helped spread this style to other parts of the country, and curry rice became everyday comfort food."},{"start":165.66,"end":170.98,"text":"The regional dish known as Yokosuka navy curry keeps that naval connection visible."},{"start":171.84,"end":180,"text":"The words “one theory” matter: food histories often have several routes, not one simple invention story."},{"start":181.26,"end":182.58,"text":"Mayonnaise followed another path."},{"start":183.32,"end":190.24,"text":"Kewpie's founder encountered mayonnaise abroad and launched Japan's first Japanese-made mayonnaise in 1925."},{"start":191.06,"end":197.9,"text":"He chose a rich egg-yolk type, and the product gradually became part of everyday Japanese food culture."},{"start":198.88,"end":202.24,"text":"In both cases, adoption was not passive copying."},{"start":202.86,"end":209.86,"text":"A foreign idea succeeded because local cooks and diners found their own uses, tastes, and meanings for it."},{"start":210.12,"end":213.62,"text":"The best sauces do not simply cover food — they complete it."},{"start":214.44,"end":218.82,"text":"This idea sounds simple, but using sauce well takes judgment."},{"start":219.5,"end":226.72,"text":"People often describe five basic taste qualities: sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami."},{"start":227.56,"end":233.04,"text":"A sauce can add or balance several of them while leaving the food underneath recognizable."},{"start":233.04,"end":237.04,"text":"Soy sauce gives grilled vegetables or tofu salt and umami."},{"start":237.88,"end":240.56,"text":"Ketchup gives fried potatoes sweetness and acidity."},{"start":241.56,"end":244.96,"text":"Mayonnaise adds richness to a dry sandwich or roasted vegetables."},{"start":245.7,"end":247.38,"text":"Amount matters as much as flavor."},{"start":248.16,"end":254.64,"text":"A little soy sauce can make the delicate taste of sashimi clearer; too much can make every bite taste only of salt."},{"start":255.24,"end":258.08,"text":"Good cooks therefore ask what the main ingredient needs."},{"start":258.74,"end":260.94,"text":"Sometimes the answer is a strong sauce."},{"start":262,"end":265.54,"text":"Sometimes it is a quiet spoonful or no sauce at all."},{"start":267.1,"end":269.1,"text":"Sauce also carries taste memory."},{"start":269.82,"end":278.4,"text":"A family's slightly sweeter curry or sharper dressing can make inexpensive rice, noodles, potatoes, or beans taste like home."},{"start":279.24,"end":284.12,"text":"The ingredients may be ordinary, but the repeated balance becomes part of identity."},{"start":284.12,"end":291.8,"text":"Sauce adds value not by pretending simple food is luxurious, but by making careful use of what is already there."}],"words":[{"text":"Some","start":0,"end":0.2},{"text":"of","start":0.2,"end":0.32},{"text":"the","start":0.32,"end":0.42},{"text":"world's","start":0.42,"end":0.74},{"text":"most","start":0.74,"end":0.88},{"text":"important","start":0.88,"end":1.3},{"text":"sauces","start":1.3,"end":1.72},{"text":"get","start":1.72,"end":2.02},{"text":"their","start":2.02,"end":2.2},{"text":"depth","start":2.2,"end":2.5},{"text":"from","start":2.5,"end":2.74},{"text":"fermentation","start":2.74,"end":3.24},{"text":"a","start":3.74,"end":3.86},{"text":"slow","start":3.86,"end":4.22},{"text":"natural","start":4.22,"end":4.6},{"text":"process","start":4.6,"end":5.1},{"text":"in","start":5.1,"end":5.42},{"text":"which","start":5.42,"end":5.64},{"text":"microbes","start":5.64,"end":6.08},{"text":"transform","start":6.08,"end":6.66},{"text":"ingredients","start":6.66,"end":7.14},{"text":"and","start":7.14,"end":7.42},{"text":"create","start":7.42,"end":7.68},{"text":"new","start":7.68,"end":7.96},{"text":"flavors","start":7.96,"end":8.34},{"text":"Fermentation","start":9.44,"end":9.7},{"text":"can","start":9.7,"end":10.22},{"text":"preserve","start":10.22,"end":10.62},{"text":"food","start":10.62,"end":11.04},{"text":"but","start":11.28,"end":11.44},{"text":"it","start":11.44,"end":11.54},{"text":"can","start":11.54,"end":11.64},{"text":"also","start":11.64,"end":11.9},{"text":"build","start":11.9,"end":12.18},{"text":"tastes","start":12.18,"end":12.64},{"text":"that","start":12.64,"end":12.92},{"text":"ordinary","start":12.92,"end":13.28},{"text":"cooking","start":13.28,"end":13.